F2XM1 outside its range (-1 to +1), where Intel documents the result as undefined, computed 2^x - 1 like any other
operand. A DOS system information program identifies the FPU by F2XM1 of pi and accepts only the answers Intel FPUs
give, one of them the operand left as it was; tiny386's 7.82 made it report a "Non-Intel" coprocessor. Now a finite
operand beyond +-1 is left as it was; in range (what exp() and pow() code use) nothing changes.

diff --git a/fpu.c b/fpu.c
--- a/fpu.c
+++ b/fpu.c
@@ -1131,6 +1131,10 @@
 			temp = fpget(fpu, 0);
 			switch (i) {
 			case 0: // F2XM1
+				/* outside -1..+1 the result is undefined: left as it was, one
+				   of an Intel FPU's answers, which CPU detectors check for */
+				if (isfinite(temp) && fabs(temp) > 1)
+					break;
 				fpset(fpu, 0, pow(2.0, temp) - 1);
 				break;
